<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Local DEVELOPMENT entitlements for the native Mac target.

  REQUESTED SET: EMPTY. This bundle asks for no entitlement at all, and that is a
  decision rather than an oversight. Each candidate is listed with why it is absent.

  Companion file: macos/Resources/Info.plist
  Built by:       macos/Scripts/package-mac-app.sh (ad-hoc identity, `codesign -s -`)

  NOTE ON A PREVIOUS REVISION
  An earlier revision of this file requested exactly one entitlement,
  com.apple.security.get-task-allow, on the grounds that it is what Xcode attaches
  to a local build so a debugger can attach. That has been REMOVED. This slice has
  no explicit debugging requirement, and the honest position is that the
  entitlement was not demonstrably necessary: this binary is ad-hoc signed WITHOUT
  hardened runtime, so debugger attach can work without it, and nothing in the
  slice depends on debugging. Asserting necessity would have been overclaiming.
  Deleting the key pair is the whole fix if a future slice adds a real debugging
  requirement and documents it.

  CANDIDATES CONSIDERED, ALL ABSENT:

  com.apple.security.get-task-allow
    Debugger attachment. No explicit debugging requirement exists for this slice.

  com.apple.security.app-sandbox
    The target writes under the user's home, reaches a caller-supplied base URL
    over HTTP, and uses the login Keychain via SessionKeychain.swift. Sandboxing
    would require further entitlements for all three and would change the native
    app's security posture for no development benefit.

  com.apple.security.network.client / .network.server
    Entitlement-only when sandboxed. Unsandboxed processes need no network
    entitlement; MusterTransport's GET-only probe works without one.

  keychain-access-groups
    SessionKeychain uses the default access group of an unsandboxed app, which
    needs no declared group. Declaring one would place development credentials in a
    shared group - the opposite of what a development bundle should do.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit, com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    Nothing in this Swift target generates or executes code at runtime, so there is
    no JIT and no unsigned executable memory to permit. The shipped Electron app
    needs both because Chromium's V8 does; copying that pair here would grant an
    exception this binary never uses.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
    The Electron app needs this to load its own unsigned helper bundles. This binary
    loads no unsigned plugin or bundle - only system frameworks and a statically
    linked CompanionCore - so the exception is unnecessary and would weaken library
    validation for no reason.

  com.apple.security.device.audio-input,
  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events, and any TCC capability
    Entitlement-only under the sandbox. The code requests no microphone, screen
    capture, accessibility or Apple Events access at all, so there is no capability
    to declare. The matching NS*UsageDescription strings are absent from Info.plist
    for the same reason.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
